<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  探索 is a real browser (spec 053), and a browser must be able to open an
	  http page: refusing would break a redirect chain that dips through http
	  and would make the local test harness unreachable.

	  It does NOT weaken the wallet. This key covers WEB CONTENT only — the
	  app's own requests still require TLS (`CoreHTTP.request` refuses anything
	  that is not https, and will keep refusing) — and `dapp_permissions`
	  refuses to sign for an insecure origin on its own, as `insecure_origin`.
	  So an http page can be READ and cannot ask for a signature.

	  `NSAllowsLocalNetworking` is for the device harness: the UI-test runner
	  serves the test dApp on 127.0.0.1:8137. `file://` is not an option, because
	  `dapp_origin_of` gives a file URL no origin at all — every signature would
	  fail closed and the harness would prove nothing.
	-->
	<!--
	  The build's commit, for 关于 (spec 058). Android reads
	  `BuildConfig.GIT_COMMIT`, which its Gradle file fills by running git at
	  configuration time. iOS cannot: `ENABLE_USER_SCRIPT_SANDBOXING = YES`
	  means a build phase may not read `.git`, and a phase that quietly failed
	  would stamp a plausible wrong hash.

	  So it is a build SETTING, defaulting to `unknown`, and a release passes
	  the real one:

	      xcodebuild archive … VELA_GIT_COMMIT=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)

	  When it is `unknown` the About page says `build <CFBundleVersion>`
	  instead — true, and never a hash nobody can look up.
	-->
	<key>VelaGitCommit</key>
	<string>$(VELA_GIT_COMMIT)</string>
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent</key>
		<true/>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<key>UIAppFonts</key>
	<array>
		<string>PlusJakartaSans_400Regular.ttf</string>
		<string>PlusJakartaSans_500Medium.ttf</string>
		<string>PlusJakartaSans_600SemiBold.ttf</string>
		<string>PlusJakartaSans_700Bold.ttf</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  保存图片 on the receive screen (spec 051): the card is rendered in the app
	  and written to the album, and nothing is ever read back. `addOnly` is the
	  narrowest authorisation Photos offers, and a wallet asking to READ
	  somebody's photos in order to save a QR code would be asking for far more
	  than it needs.
	-->
	<!--
	  The scanner (spec 055): reading a payment code, a receive code, or a
	  WalletConnect-style URI with the camera. The system shows this sentence
	  the first time the viewfinder is opened, and never again — so it says what
	  the camera is FOR rather than that a camera exists.
	-->
	<!--
	  `velawallet://` (spec 056). Two shapes reach the app: `pay` prefills a
	  send, `open` hands a URL to the in-app browser. Universal links to
	  `getvela.app` ride the `applinks:` entitlement, which has been there since
	  019 with nothing behind it.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>app.getvela.VelaWallet</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Editor</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>velawallet</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Vela uses the camera to scan QR codes — a payment request, or somebody's address.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Vela saves your receive QR code to your album so you can share it.</string>
	<!--
	  Two uses, one sentence — iOS shows this key once, for whichever comes
	  first, so it has to be true of both:

	    - caBLE "sign in with your phone" (spec 019): the BLE SCAN that finds
	      the phone that scanned our QR, and the CTAP 2.3 L2CAP channel to it;
	    - the Clear Signer's nearby channel (spec 075 T041): this phone
	      ADVERTISING as a GATT peripheral so the signing page open on a
	      computer in the room can connect to it (PROTOCOL.md §1).

	  The peripheral role is what makes the wording matter: asking for it is
	  what raises the prompt, and a sentence that only mentioned scanning would
	  be asking to advertise under cover of a different explanation.
	-->
	<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Vela uses Bluetooth to reach a nearby device you sign with — the phone that scans the code on screen, or a computer running the Clear Signer in the same room.</string>
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>fetch</string>
	</array>
	<key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>app.getvela.VelaWallet.tracker</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
